# Template for .secrets.production — copy this file, fill in real values, and
# keep the copy out of Git:
#
#   cp .secrets.production.example .secrets.production
#
# `pnpm deploy` uploads .secrets.production to the Worker via
# `wrangler secret bulk` before building, so every var here becomes a
# Cloudflare Worker secret rather than a value baked into the deployed bundle.
#
# What lives here vs. in wrangler.jsonc's `vars`:
#   - Here: anything sensitive (credentials, signing keys) OR anything whose
#     correct value differs per deployed environment (origins, per-store IDs).
#   - wrangler.jsonc `vars`: non-sensitive settings that are the same for
#     every production deployment (DATA_MODE, CACHE_ENABLED, etc.). Those are
#     committed and readable in plaintext, so nothing secret belongs there.
#
# The var names here are mirrored in wrangler.jsonc's `secrets.required`, which
# is what makes `wrangler dev` warn about a missing one instead of failing at
# runtime. Adding a secret means adding it in both places.

# ===== BIGCOMMERCE APP CREDENTIALS =====
# Sensitive. From Dev Portal > your app > OAuth. The client secret both
# exchanges auth codes for tokens and verifies BigCommerce's inbound
# signed_payload_jwt, so leaking it forges app installs.
BIGCOMMERCE_CLIENT_ID=
BIGCOMMERCE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ===== DEPLOYMENT IDENTITY =====
# Not sensitive, but not universal either — it differs for every deployed
# environment (production vs. a staging Worker vs. a preview URL), which is
# why it can't live in the committed `vars` block. Must exactly match the
# origin registered as your app's callback base in the Dev Portal.
# Example: https://starter-sc-app-next.example.workers.dev
APP_ORIGIN=

# ===== SIGNING AND ENCRYPTION KEYS =====
# Sensitive. Signs/verifies this app's own session cookie — deliberately a
# different secret from BIGCOMMERCE_CLIENT_SECRET, since it guards a different
# trust boundary (our session vs. BigCommerce's inbound payload).
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
SESSION_SECRET=

# Sensitive. Symmetric key used to encrypt stored store access tokens at rest,
# so a dump of the credentials store isn't a dump of usable API tokens.
# Rotating this makes previously stored tokens undecryptable.
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
CREDENTIALS_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# ===== STATIC MODE STORE CREDENTIALS =====
# Only used when DATA_MODE=STATIC (set in wrangler.jsonc's `vars`), where every
# request uses this one store's API token instead of looking a token up per
# user from durable storage. Useful for prototyping a deployed Worker against
# a single real store before the MULTITENANT install flow is wired up.
#
# STATIC_STORE_TOKEN is why these belong here rather than in `vars`: it's a
# real BigCommerce API token granting full access to the store below, and
# anything in `vars` is committed in plaintext.
#
# Leave both empty in MOCK or MULTITENANT mode — they're unread there.
# `wrangler secret bulk` uploads empty values as empty secrets rather than
# skipping them, which is harmless, but you can also delete these two lines
# from your .secrets.production if you're not using STATIC mode.
STATIC_STORE_HASH=
STATIC_STORE_TOKEN=
